 "verdict": "No named astrophysical direction lies within the ~30-40 deg localization radius of any recovered axis.  Nearest to the cap-declaration axis is the south galactic pole at 35.6 deg, and the closest approach in the bipolar sense is the published Planet Nine perihelion direction (244.6,+15.8): 150 deg from the primary axis but 29.8 deg from its antipode (14.2 deg from the detached axis antipode) -- inside the bipolar field's mirror lobe, the location the anti-aligned shepherding picture itself predicts for the perturber.  For the detached axis that antipodal consistency is expected rather than coincidental -- the axis and the P9 direction derive from the same TNO clustering under the anti-aligned convention -- while for the comet-side cap axis the closest named approach is consistent with chance (p=0.71 under a uniform-direction null): the axis is a measured structure, not a named direction of the coordinate sky.",
 "caveats": [
  "Separations are geometric only: proximity to a named direction would not by itself convict that mechanism (the galactic-tide insertion controls in steps 070/085 already bound the tide's dynamical effect at ~1e-8 deg per comet).",
  "The coincidence null treats the named set as fixed; the choice of which directions count as 'named' is itself a mild look-elsewhere factor and is reported as such."
 ]
}